meegram (0.3.5) unstable; urgency=low

  * Reactions. The pills sit inside the bubble, under the message: each one is the emoji,
    how many people have used it, and whether one of them is you. Tapping a pill adds
    your reaction or takes it back, holding a message offers a grid of twelve to pick one
    that is not there yet, and All reactions lists who reacted and with what - a large
    group only lets its admins ask that, and says so rather than showing an empty list.
  * Names in a group are coloured, the same person keeping the same colour everywhere, and
    whoever runs the group is marked beside their name: their own title if they have been
    given one, owner or admin if they have not.
  * A channel you only subscribe to no longer shows a composer. It used to offer a send
    that Telegram then refused; now the whole row goes and the messages take the space
    back. Channels carry an icon in the chat list so they can be told apart at a glance.
  * Emoji in a chat title or in a sender's name are drawn as emoji instead of being left
    as the raw characters, and a long name with one in it is now cut to fit its row -
    marked-up text ignores the ordinary way of doing that, so it overran.
  * A chat no longer opens showing its last message alone. Telegram answers the first
    request for history out of whatever it is already holding, which can be a single
    message, and that answer was taken at face value; too short an answer is now asked
    again rather than believed.
  * Mention autocomplete shows each person's name beside their username, and somebody with
    no username at all can now be picked - their name goes into the message as ordinary
    words, with the pointer alongside it that makes it a real mention. Two people with the
    same name, or one mentioned twice, each get their own.
  * The @ mark on a chat in the list is cleared once the message that put it there has
    been read. It never cleared: reading a chat moves a single "read up to here" pointer,
    which is not what clears a mention - the message carrying it has to be named - so the
    mark stayed for good.
  * Opening a profile from a group's member list, or a conversation from a profile, pushes
    a new page instead of rewriting the page you were on and then pushing a copy of it.
    Going back now returns to what you were looking at, and a conversation left open
    behind a profile is still that conversation when you come back to it.
  * Creating a group left you sitting on the chat list. The new group opens.
  * The group members list shows a spinner while it is being fetched, rather than the
    section appearing from nowhere once it lands.
  * A contact's phone number can be tapped to call it, where the number is one you are
    allowed to see.
  * Save to gallery on a batch of photos, and the banner after a file is saved, showed
    their own key names instead of any translation. Neither key existed in Telegram's
    language pack; both were guesses.

 -- Andrea Faggin <hellothere@andreafagg.in>  Tue, 11 Aug 2026 18:30:00 +0200

meegram (0.3.4) unstable; urgency=low

  * Several photos at once. Tapping a thumbnail in the gallery now ticks it instead of
    sending it straight away, and the toolbar sends everything ticked as one batch - a
    mosaic at the other end, the same way an incoming one is drawn. Ten at a time, which
    is Telegram's own limit on a batch.
  * Several files at once, the same way, from the file browser. Ticks survive browsing
    into another folder and back, so a set can be gathered from more than one place.
  * Tapping somebody's @name in a message opens their profile, whether or not you have
    ever written to them - it did nothing at all before. From there, Send message opens
    the conversation with them.
  * Typing @ in a group offers the members it could be, and picking one completes it.
    Only members with a username are offered: mentioning somebody without one takes a
    kind of message this client cannot send yet.
  * Fixed a crash from 0.3.3: tapping the quote on a reply to a photo closed the app.
    The jump rebuilt the conversation while the message that had been tapped was still
    animating, and the animation went on writing to it after it was gone.
  * The message you land on after tapping a quote now marks itself, which it was meant
    to do in 0.3.3 and never did: it dimmed the balloon behind the words rather than the
    message, and it did so while the list was still moving to it.

 -- Andrea Faggin <hellothere@andreafagg.in>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 20:45:00 +0200

meegram (0.3.3) unstable; urgency=low

  * Photos sent together are drawn as one bubble, laid out as a mosaic, the way the
    official clients do it. A set of eight photos used to arrive as eight bubbles and
    filled the screen on its own.
  * The text sent with a batch of photos is no longer lost. Telegram attaches it to one
    photo of the batch rather than to all of them, and that was never the one the bubble
    read, so the photos arrived and the words did not.
  * Replying by swipe: push a message to the right and it becomes the reply. Anywhere on
    the bubble, on any photo of an album, or on the quote of a message that is itself a
    reply. Holding a message still offers Reply as before.
  * Tapping the quote at the top of a reply jumps to the message it points at, and that
    message dims and comes back so it can be picked out. If it is further back than the
    part of the conversation already loaded, the chat pages back through its history to
    find it instead of saying it cannot.
  * Photos open at full size. The viewer showed the copy that had been downloaded for the
    bubble, which only has to cover a 480-pixel screen, so zooming into it was soft. It
    now fills the page with that copy immediately and swaps to the real photo as soon as
    it has been fetched.
  * Saving a photo from the viewer, as an action on its toolbar. It saves the full-size
    photo, not the copy on screen. A whole batch can be saved at once with Save all
    photos when holding an album, one photo at a time so a dozen downloads do not go out
    together.
  * A message arriving while you are reading further up no longer drags the conversation
    down to it. It waits at the bottom, where it was already going.
  * Opening the keyboard no longer pushes the newest message behind the text field. The
    same thing happened when the reply banner appeared above the composer.

 -- Andrea Faggin <hellothere@andreafagg.in>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 19:30:00 +0200

meegram (0.3.2) unstable; urgency=low

  * Tapping a notification opens the chat in about four seconds, where it used to take
    twenty-seven. Nothing was slow in the way it looked: the app could not open the
    tapped chat until the background service had handed over its entire session — every
    chat, every contact, five and a half megabytes of it — because that was the only
    thing that put the chat within reach. It now asks for the one chat it was told to
    open, and gets it in ten milliseconds.
  * Opening a chat by name rather than from the list — Saved Messages, a contact you
    have never written to, a tapped notification — no longer depends on the app having
    been started before the background service. It could quietly do nothing at all.
  * The app starts lighter and stays lighter: about 20 MB less memory at the moment a
    chat opens, because the session no longer arrives as one enormous message that has
    to be taken apart in one piece.
  * The language pack is no longer downloaded on every single launch. It is 1.8 MB, it
    changes a few times a year, and every launch spent seconds on it before showing
    anything. It is now refreshed every couple of days instead.

 -- Andrea Faggin <hellothere@andreafagg.in>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 17:45:00 +0200

meegram (0.3.1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Starting a conversation with somebody who is not in the chat list yet. A new-message
    page searches your contacts by name or username and everybody else by their public
    username, and opens a chat with whoever you pick. The search field over the chat list
    only ever matched chats Telegram had already sent; this one asks the server.
  * A contact you have never written to can now be opened. There was no chat to open —
    Telegram creates one on demand — and asking for it by id simply answered "not found",
    which also affected Saved Messages on a fresh install and notifications tapped before
    the chat list had loaded.
  * Finding which of the phone's contacts are on Telegram, from the new-message page.
    It says what it does before it does it: the numbers are sent to Telegram and the
    matches are added to your Telegram contacts on every device signed into the account.
    The official clients do the same thing on first launch without asking.
  * Making a group: a name, a list of contacts to tick, and the group opens once the
    server has it. Ticks survive filtering the list, so you can search for each person in
    turn. A basic group, as the official clients create; Telegram upgrades it to a
    supergroup on its own when it outgrows one.
  * Search results carry the person's photo. A profile photo belongs to the person rather
    than to any one conversation, so it now lives on the user — a contact with no chat had
    nowhere to keep one. Avatars nobody has ever fetched are downloaded on sight and fill
    themselves in as they land.
  * A chat with no messages in it no longer spins forever. The spinner was waiting on a
    row count that never comes for an empty chat — one just created, or one that was
    cleared — and it now gives way to "still no messages".

 -- Andrea Faggin <blackbird5308@gmail.com>  Sun, 02 Aug 2026 16:00:00 +0100

meegram (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * Voice notes. Tap the microphone to start, tap again to send; the attach button
    becomes discard while it records, and a counter runs beside it. They play back in
    the app, in the bubble. Recorded, encoded and decoded in-process as OggOpus, which
    neither the device image nor its GStreamer 0.10 has.
  * Video and GIF messages show as a bubble with the still and a badge giving the
    runtime and the size — "GIF" in place of the runtime for an animation. Only the
    still is fetched on sight; the first tap downloads the video and the second hands
    it to whatever Harmattan has registered for the type. Nothing plays in the chat.
  * Documents, sent from a file picker over MyDocs and received with their name and
    size. Not downloaded on sight: the first tap fetches, the second opens it in
    whatever the device has for the type, and long press saves it under the sender's
    filename into MyDocs/meegram/Downloads. Audio messages render the same way.
  * An emoji picker under the message field, across seven category tabs.
  * A profile page from the chat header: the large photo, username, bio and phone
    number, for the person or group you are talking to.
  * Delivery state on outgoing messages: a clock while sending, a tick once the
    server has it, a green one once it has been read, and a marker when it failed.
  * Search over the chat list, matching titles as you type. It pulls the rest of the
    list in while a search is running, since the list otherwise pages by the screenful.
  * Sending a message no longer left a duplicate of it behind once the server
    acknowledged it under its real id.
  * A chat whose first page of history was shorter than the screen no longer stops
    paging altogether — there was nothing to flick, so nothing asked for more, and it
    stayed that way until the chat was closed and reopened.
  * A search matching nothing now says so instead of showing a spinner for five
    seconds, and a row updated while filtered out no longer comes back stale when the
    search is cleared.
  * Build: opus is built fixed-point for the device, with its ARM intrinsics off —
    opus's CMake build advertises a NEON kernel it never assembles, so linking failed
    on an undefined celt_pitch_xcorr_neon. libogg and opus also needed a policy
    minimum to configure under CMake 4.

 -- Andrea Faggin <blackbird5308@gmail.com>  Sat, 01 Aug 2026 16:00:00 +0100

meegram (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Notifications now come from meegramd, a resident daemon that owns the TDLib
    connection and its database lock. Closing the app no longer closes the Telegram
    connection, so messages still arrive and still notify; tapping one opens that
    chat, starting the app if it was closed.
  * The daemon starts on demand through D-Bus activation, and is stopped on upgrade
    so the newly installed binary is the one that comes back.
  * Notifications follow TDLib's own notification updates, so per-chat and scope
    settings are honoured, outgoing and service messages are skipped, and reading a
    message on another device withdraws its banner here.
  * The app talks to the daemon over a per-session socket, one JSON object per line.
  * First launch no longer hangs on the loading screen: the language pack is asked
    for only once TDLib has accepted its parameters, is cached on disk for the next
    start, and startup stops waiting for it after a deadline rather than spinning
    with no way to sign in.
  * Opening a chat lands on the newest message again, rather than the oldest one
    loaded, and scrolling back keeps paging through history.
  * Starting the app while the daemon is already running no longer comes up to an
    empty chat list that never fills. The app now asks TDLib for the state it missed,
    which also restores folder tabs, sender names and the connection indicator.
  * Minimising the app and returning to a chat no longer makes messages vanish or
    appear twice.
  * Fixed a crash when a page of history arrived while the message list was being
    drawn.
  * Faster chat list: avatars are cached in memory and kept masked on disk between
    runs, the list no longer relays out when nothing has moved, download progress
    no longer re-runs every binding on a row, and message rows keep their formatted
    text.
  * Optional on-device scope timing, -DMEEGRAM_PROFILE=ON, described in
    docs/profiling.md.

 -- Andrea Faggin <blackbird5308@gmail.com>  Sat, 01 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0100

meegram (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * Message actions on long press: reply, copy, edit, delete.
  * Chat actions on long press: pin, mark as read/unread, mute, delete or leave.
  * System notifications with the chat avatar; tapping one opens that chat, and
    starts the app over D-Bus activation if it was closed.
  * Photo messages render, and can be sent from the gallery with a caption.
  * Stickers: animated ones through rlottie, static ones through libwebp.
  * Read receipts are reported, so other clients and the sender see messages read.
  * Typing and recording indicators in the chat header.
  * Chat avatars now update when their download finishes, and are masked and
    cropped once in C++ rather than per row in QML.
  * Emoji assets resized to the size they are drawn at: 19.6 MB down to 7.8 MB.
  * Chat list shows every chat, not only pinned ones, and pages on demand.
  * Chats open on the newest message or the unread marker, and follow new messages
    unless you have scrolled up.
  * Fixed message deletion, which asked for a vector of several billion elements.
  * Fixed groups never loading history when they had never been opened.

